WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.030
- [Attorney] Thanks to CuriosityStream

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.370
for keeping LegalEagle in the air.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.650
I don't know about you, but it seems like

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.120
every celebrity wants their
own trademark these days.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.940
The summer of 2019 was filled with

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.730
such celebrity trademark news

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.710
as Tom Brady wanting to
trademark Tom Terrific

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.050
for some reason.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:17.630
LeBron James filed an application

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.010
to register the trademark
for Taco Tuesday.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.260
And Lizzo says she's the only one

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.890
who can call herself 100% that bitch.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.950
Sure, it's all fun and
games until LeBron James

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:28.900
takes away your right to send pictures

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.882
of your food with the hashtag
Taco Tuesday to your friends.

00:00:31.882 --> 00:00:34.900
- [Sports Announcer] Here
comes LeBron, he blocks it!

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.980
- So, what is going on with celebrities

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.855
and the U.S. Patent and
Trademark Office, anyway?

00:00:39.855 --> 00:00:42.605
(dramatic music)

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.393
- Hey, LegalEagles!

00:00:44.393 --> 00:00:46.750
Today we are talking about
celebrity trademarks.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.780
But first, what is a trademark?

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.690
A trademark is a word, symbol, or phrase

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.830
that's used to identify
a particular manufacturer

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.060
or a particular product
to distinguish them from

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.190
the products of another company or person.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.480
For example, Coke and
Pepsi have different logos.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.390
That's how you can be
assured that you are grabbing

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.420
a delicious Diet Coke rather than

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.730
a clearly inferior Diet Pepsi.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.060
Though obviously, we can all agree

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.620
that Dr Pepper is by far the
best carbonated soda out there.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.820
Now, the federal law that
protects trademarks used

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.870
in commerce is called the Lanham Act.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.440
The U.S. Patent and Trademark Office

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.720
or USPTO for short, is in charge

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.930
of granting federal trademark protection.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.290
Trademarks are often
confused with copyright,

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.110
but they're two very different concepts

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.670
and they cover different rights.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.420
Copyright protects original works

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.310
of creative authorship
fixed in a tangible medium.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.130
That means that authors and artists

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.050
have the exclusive rights
to do whatever they want

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.129
with the things that they make
for a limited amount of time.

00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:42.620
Whether that's a book, a song, a movie,

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.450
a photograph, a sculpture,
or anything else

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.970
that qualifies for copyright protection.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.540
For example, if you write a novel,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.420
you don't need to
register your book or ask

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.280
the government to provided
a certificate proving

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.170
you wrote it.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.010
Once your book is fixed
in a tangible medium,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.640
in other words it's
written down in some form,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.570
it's protected by copyright law

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.140
and no one else can copy your book

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.600
or adapt it for the big
screen, for instance,

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.010
without your permission.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.900
Trademarks on the other
hand, are very different.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.530
They don't cover creative
expression, per se.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.700
A trademark is not about rewarding

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:13.730
the trademark holder.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.130
The goal of trademark
protection is not just

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.490
to protect the person or business from

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.950
having their trademark stolen,

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.640
it's all about preventing
consumer confusion.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.657
If you own the rights
to a certain trademark,

00:02:23.657 --> 00:02:26.070
you can sue someone else
from using a trademark

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.760
that seems similar to
yours to prevent consumers

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.020
from being confused.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.210
To prove that someone
infringed on your trademark,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.710
you need to show that it's likely

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.380
to cause consumer confusion
about the source of

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.330
the product or service at issue.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:39.390
Let me give you an example;

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.830
Adidas has trademarked it's
distinctive three stripe logo

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.590
which it places on everything from shoes

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:46.410
to hats to track pants.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.830
Forever 21 sells trendy
clothes including sometimes

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.493
clothes that seem to have
the same three stripe pattern

00:02:52.493 --> 00:02:55.670
that you would usually
find on Adidas clothes.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.940
Adidas sued for copyright
infringement in 2017.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.740
The problem was that the striped sweats

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.360
that Forever 21 was selling,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.610
it looked exactly like
authentic Adidas clothes.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.360
It looked like something
Adidas would actually produce.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.310
The parties eventually
settled out of court

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.150
but it's very likely that Adidas would

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.240
have won that suit.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.900
With a trademark the point is to sure

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.480
that consumers know who
made the product or service.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.640
When you see the familiar
Adidas stripe pattern

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.180
on clothes, you know what
kinds of products they sell

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.790
and the values associated
with that particular brand.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.010
You can also trademark
phrases and packaging.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.706
Things like short phrases
and even single words

00:03:30.706 --> 00:03:33.030
that would never be protected by copyright

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.610
because they're too short
and not creative enough,

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.480
that actually might qualify
for trademark protection,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.160
again, for the purpose of
preventing consumer confusion.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.490
For a mark to qualify for registration

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.750
with the trademark office,
it needs to be distinctive.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.670
This is based on how well
the trademark identifies

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.990
the source or manufacturer of
a certain product or service.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.270
There are four types of distinctive marks;

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:57.899
fanciful marks are inherently
distinctive because

00:03:57.899 --> 00:04:00.070
they have no meaning outside

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:02.030
of that which is gained in the context

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.710
of selling a good or service.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.010
The word Google for example,
doesn't mean anything.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.080
It's just a made-up name
that is now associated

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.170
with an internet service provider.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.150
The same is true of
Kodak, Xerox, or Exxon.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.960
These are the strongest
kinds of trademarks

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.570
because they don't have
any meaning outside

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:18.670
of the trademark itself.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.540
The next strongest form of
mark is an arbitrary mark

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.000
which is something that
already has a common meaning,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.180
but doesn't have a connection to the goods

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.730
or services being sold.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.600
Apple is a fruit but it's also a computer.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.480
Lotus is a software but it's also a plant.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.330
These are highly protectable because

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.980
they are arbitrary compared
to the thing being sold.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.820
Next, there are suggestive
marks which refer

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.780
to some quality or
characteristic of the good.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.680
A jaguar, for example, is a
name of a super sleek cat.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.750
But, that's also why the car company chose

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.030
that name to associate the characteristics

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.330
of the cat with it's cars.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.410
And then finally, we have
descriptive trademarks

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.740
which simply describe
the service or goods.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:00.040
A descriptive trademark is weak since

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.090
it doesn't do anything to identify

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:03.940
the source of the product or service

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:06.870
or, it's pretty hard to
create that identification.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.060
If something is merely descriptive,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.050
it will not qualify for registration.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:12.950
So, for example, if I start a bowl company

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.310
named Cereal Bowl, it's unlikely the USPTO

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:17.920
would grant me a trademark.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.810
I can still use Cereal
Bowl for my company name

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.090
but, I'd have a hard time stopping others

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.240
from using it because it's
merely a descriptive mark.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.700
But, if I called a new
clothing company Cereal Bowl,

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.330
that would be an arbitrary mark

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.140
and could be very strong because

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.810
why would you associate
the words cereal bowl

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:36.930
with a clothing company?

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:38.580
It's arbitrary, and it's very likely

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.310
to be able to identify
Cereal Bowl clothes.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.260
Oh, God, I'm so hungry for some cereal

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.530
and some Dr Pepper right now.

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.770
In addition to the
distinctiveness requirement

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.027
for any particular mark,

00:05:48.027 --> 00:05:50.660
you also have to use the mark in commerce

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.430
before you can get
protection from the USPTO.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.570
Trademark has sort of a use
it or lose it provision.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:59.520
Now, in some cases you
can declare your intent

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.565
to use a mark and get protection

00:06:01.565 --> 00:06:04.070
but, that's temporary
and still requires use

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.710
in commerce in the short-term.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.680
Now, say you have two similar marks.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.540
The question is whether
there would be a likelihood

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.230
of consumer confusion
between the two marks.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.650
On top of that, even if the two marks

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.050
are very, very similar,

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.950
in fact, sometimes they can be identical

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:19.920
using exactly the same words.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.976
A likelihood of confusion
will only be found to exist

00:06:22.976 --> 00:06:25.610
where the goods or the underlying services

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.890
are in fact connected or related.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.900
What most people get wrong about trademark

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.720
is that a trademark
only covers a narrow set

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.290
of goods or services.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.110
Just because you have a
trademark over a specific word

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:38.780
doesn't mean you can monopolize

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.570
that word throughout the entire universe.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.000
You only get potential
control over similar goods

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.480
or services that use a confusing
similar mark in commerce.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.920
That's why Wolf Ranges
can exist at the same time

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.320
as Wolf Clothing.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.010
Or, why Apple Records which
was started by the Beatles

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.580
could exist at the same
time as Apple Computers

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.400
which was started by Steve Jobs.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.080
Though, that's probably
not a great example

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.650
because Apple Records and
Apple Computers sued each other

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:04.920
for decades.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.050
Although, maybe it is
a good example because

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.240
in the 1970s, computers
didn't really have anything

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.730
to do with music so
there was no real overlap

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.800
between Apple Records and Apple Computers.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.620
But, as computers became
able to make and play music,

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:20.480
overlap was created.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.510
Especially when Apple
Computers started making iPods

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:25.520
and the iTunes Music Store.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.380
At that point, it was very possible

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.220
that consumers might be
confused between the two marks.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.070
For example, if you heard
the phrase Apple Music

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:36.710
in 2005, odds are you
probably thought of Steve Jobs

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:37.990
rather than Paul McCartney.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.380
And, that's why Apple
Records and Apple Computers

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.330
were in lawsuits with each other for

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.030
the better part of 40 years.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:44.863
Here's another example,

00:07:44.863 --> 00:07:46.300
since I have tennis on the brain since

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.100
the US Open is currently on.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.590
Roger Federer is one of the most popular

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:52.960
and famous tennis players of all time.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.890
His name and likeness are
powerful for selling goods

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:57.980
and it gives him the ability
to get paid big bucks

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.610
to endorse products like
Rolex, Lindt Chocolates,

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.060
UNIQLO, and Nike.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.390
If Roger Federer could
not protect his name

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:06.430
and likeness, then maybe I could show up

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.240
at the US Open selling special socks

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.570
supposedly endorsed by Roger Federer.

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:13.860
And, I'm sure I'd be able
to sell a lot of them.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.010
Trademarks are about
preventing market confusion.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.860
My Federer socks could
hoodwink a lot of people.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.360
And, it's exactly that
reason why the USPTO

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:24.040
denied trademark registration

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.620
for Rob Kardashian's ex, Blac Chyna

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:29.920
when she tried to take
the Kardashian name.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.030
Blac Chyna tried to register
the name Angela Kardashian

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.250
when she was engaged to Rob Kardashian.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:37.640
His sisters successfully
blocked her attempt

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.180
to register that trademark.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.920
The various Kardashian companies

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.940
and Kardashian people have powerful brands

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.080
that have generated hundreds
of millions of dollars.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.140
The USPTO agreed with the Kardashians

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.200
that if Blac Chyna
started endorsing products

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.040
with the name Angela Kardashian,

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.670
the product would inevitably
be considered part of

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:56.450
the Kardashian brand.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.830
In particular, that's
called trademark dilution

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.390
because you are diluting the power of

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:02.510
that particular mark.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:03.840
And, in that particular instance,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.130
that's probably considered
trademark passing-off

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.410
where you're trying to pass off your goods

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:10.700
as being affiliated with somebody else.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:12.860
And, both of those are no-nos as far

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:13.970
as trademark law goes.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:15.930
But, it was easy to see why Blac Chyna

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.840
was eager to capitalize on that name

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:19.360
and it was also easy to see why the

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.310
Kardashians wanted to stop her.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:22.810
So, let's talk about some of these

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.870
celebrity trademark names
that are in the news today.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.350
As the would be Angela
Kardashian recognized,

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.180
registering a name has
become increasingly popular.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.830
However, to really enjoy the protection

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.360
of trademark law, you have to promise

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.810
to use that name in commerce.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.390
If you use the name you're
trademark will disappear.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.030
You'll abandon that trademark.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:42.520
Many celebrities have been criticized

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.650
for trying to trademark their kid's names.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.440
They usually say that
they have no intention

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:49.390
of ever making money on
the name of their child,

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:51.290
they just want to stop other people

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:54.070
from capitalizing on
their children's name.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.240
But, this legal tactic
doesn't really work.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.780
Since trademarks are given specifically

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.380
for use in commerce.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.730
You have to use them to get a trademark.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:05.580
And, even then they would only apply

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.240
to similar goods.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:08.830
Are children goods?

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:10.730
No, no, they are not.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.990
So, it didn't go well
for Beyoncé and Jay-Z

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.610
when they tried to register the mark

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.760
for their daughter Blue Ivy.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.320
A Boston company called
Blue Ivy Elements beat them

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.050
to the punch demonstrating
that they had used

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.400
the mark Blue Ivy since at least 2009.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.600
And, four years later,
the Carters tried again

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.170
this time trying to
register Blue Ivy Carter.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:31.060
But, they insisted the
reason that they needed

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:33.520
the trademark was to stop
others from using it.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.390
Once again, Blue Ivy
Events contested the filing

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.320
and they accused the
Carters of trademark fraud

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.660
since they claim to have no
intention of actually using

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.980
the name for goods or services.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.490
And, once again, the Carters were defeated

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:49.520
because you can't just try to
monopolize a particular name.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.220
Which brings us to whether
Tom Brady can trademark

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:53.790
the term Tom Terrific.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:57.200
Nicknames actually can be
eligible for trademark.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:59.780
That was clearly what Tom
Brady thought when he tried

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.850
to file the trademark registration for

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.440
the nickname Tom Terrific.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.120
When Brady's registration
attempt became public,

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.730
he backpedaled claiming that
he just want to stop people

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.310
from associating the name with him

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:11.690
because he thought it was stupid.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.260
- I was actually trying to do something

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.410
'cause I didn't like
the nickname and I want

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.240
to make sure no one used it
'cause some people wanted

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:19.073
to use it.

00:11:19.073 --> 00:11:19.906
- What?

00:11:19.906 --> 00:11:21.180
That makes absolutely no legal sense.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.190
When you file a trademark you have to show

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:24.527
that it is closely associated with

00:11:24.527 --> 00:11:27.470
the thing you intend to use
it for in the marketplace.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:28.710
Not that you are hoping

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.070
that people will never
call you Tom Terrific

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:33.120
and that you want the
trademark specifically

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.620
to stop people from using it

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.180
and, that you yourself will never use

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:39.170
it because you hate that
particular nickname.

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.550
I would love to hear the
communications between Brady

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.507
and his lawyer. Did he
just call him up and say,

00:11:43.507 --> 00:11:47.250
"Hey, some guy on ESPN
saw my comeback last week

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.137
and is now calling me Tom Terrific."

00:11:49.137 --> 00:11:51.250
"Send that guy a cease and desist letter!"

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:54.420
Not surprisingly, we already
know what the USPTO thinks

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.070
of this particular Brady
trademark registration

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.530
because it has already
denied the application.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:03.220
The USPTO not only noticed
that Brady had no intent

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:06.160
to really use it in commerce,
but also the nickname

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.770
includes a matter which may
falsely suggest a connection

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:10.200
to Tom Seaver.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.320
Tom Seaver for those
of you who are younger

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.520
than the age of 70 is a former
Major League Baseball pitcher

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.100
who started for the 1969 Mets.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:19.700
He was so good that the media gave him

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.520
the nicknames Tom Terrific
and The Franchise.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.597
Because of that, the
USPTO held that quote,

00:12:25.597 --> 00:12:29.140
"The mark Tom Terrific points
uniquely and unmistakably

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.490
to Tom Seaver and the fame or reputation

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:33.930
of Tom Seaver as 'Tom Terrific' is such

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:35.610
that a connection between Mr. Seaver

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.610
and the applied-for
goods would be presumed."

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:40.170
Again, the USPTO is telling us

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.350
that consumer confusion is key.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:44.090
Products featuring the nickname

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:46.730
could make you think Seaver
endorsed or approved them.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:48.920
And, the USPTO also thought
that is was significant

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.920
that Seaver is alive and didn't consent

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:53.360
to the Brady trademark registration.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.260
This is the kind of
misuse of the legal system

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:56.620
that really grinds my gears.

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:58.830
Brady's attempt to grab the trademark

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:01.110
was an attempt to control speech.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:04.560
He just wanted to not
call him Tom Terrific.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.630
But frankly, I don't think
we can let this stand.

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.210
Even though Brady is on
my Fantasy Football team,

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:10.810
let's start a movement to start calling

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:14.450
him Tom Terrific Brady,
hastag TomTerrificBrady.

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:18.110
Which brings us to famous
catchphrases like Taco Tuesday.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.730
It is possible to trademark
certain catchphrases.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.990
But, only for the protection of it's use

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:24.700
in connection with goods or services.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.410
Just saying a catchphrase doesn't make it

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:27.990
something that you can register.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.380
But, you can use the
catchphrase in connection

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:31.620
with goods and services.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.590
You can also stop someone
else from profiting

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:35.980
from your catchphrase
in a similar industry.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:37.580
When it comes to registering catchphrases,

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.830
Taylor Swift is a frequent filer.

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:41.570
She has filed trademarks applications

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:43.630
for the names of her
albums like Reputation.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.480
Lyrics from her songs like,
"The old Taylor can't come

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.050
to the phone right now," and even

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:51.770
the name of her world tours
like the 1989 World Tour.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:53.560
But, Swift doesn't always win registration

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:54.400
for her trademarks.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.290
That's because in order
to get them you need

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:58.220
to prove that it's more than just a thing

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:00.200
that she said one time.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:01.690
Which brings us to LeBron.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:04.520
Can LeBron James register Taco Tuesday?

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.230
One of LeBron's companies
filed a trademark application

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:09.010
on August 15th stating that he intended

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.450
to use the term Taco Tuesday
in his business ventures.

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:14.114
LeBron has had a fun summer Instagramming

00:14:14.114 --> 00:14:17.470
his family's enjoyment
of weekly taco meals.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:21.810
- It's Tuesday somewhere,
so you know what that means?

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:26.770
It's Taco Tuesss-daaay!

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:28.610
- So much fun that his
company says he intends

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:30.380
to start an empire of products

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.690
and podcast using the phrase.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.290
But, LeBron's quest maybe
is doomed to failure

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.610
as his first season as a Laker.

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:38.443
That's right.

00:14:38.443 --> 00:14:39.276
I said it.

00:14:39.276 --> 00:14:40.850
A Wyoming restaurant
called Taco Johns' got

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:44.060
the Taco Tuesday trademark
way back in 1982.

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:46.540
Taco John's is a small western franchise

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.120
but it has defended it's
trademark with some success.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:51.800
However, the trademark is
considered relatively weak since

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:52.930
it's merely descriptive.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.710
It isn't fanciful or
original, it's a phrase

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.710
that many people have used for decades

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:00.290
to describe the fact
that they're eating tacos

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:01.440
on Tuesdays.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.270
And, it's not necessarily
something that you hear

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:04.103
and immediately think,

00:15:04.103 --> 00:15:06.510
"I should go to Taco John's right now."

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:08.182
That's probably why restaurants everywhere

00:15:08.182 --> 00:15:11.450
have used Taco Tuesdays in promotions.

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:13.520
So, LeBron is starting with two strikes.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:15.550
One, is that another company already uses

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.130
the trademark Taco Tuesday,

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.920
and two, the trademark is
descriptive of something

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:20.753
that people do.

00:15:20.753 --> 00:15:23.260
They eat a bunch of tacos on Tuesday.

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:26.420
It isn't truly associated
with LeBron or his products

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:27.730
in any particular way.

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:29.550
As a result, the USPTO is likely

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.910
to deny LeBron's trademark the same way

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:33.240
that they denied Brady.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.350
So, finally that takes us to Lizzo.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.560
Lizzo is taking the
world by storm in 2019,

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:38.760
so the singer decided to take things

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:40.840
to the next level by applying for

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:43.030
the trademark 100% that bitch

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:44.665
from her song Truth Hurts.

00:15:44.665 --> 00:15:49.665
♪ I just took a DNA test, turns
out I'm 100% that bitch. ♪

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.050
Lizzo says she intends to
use the phrase on clothing

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:54.690
and entertainment services.

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:57.040
Some people have called
out Lizzo because she

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:58.770
didn't invent the phrase.

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:00.470
As far as anyone knows the first person

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:02.650
to gain traction with that
phrase on the internet

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:06.497
was singer Mina Lioness who
tweeted back in 2017 quote,

00:16:06.497 --> 00:16:10.520
"I did a DNA test and I found
out I'm 100% that bitch."

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.740
Which, I probably don't need
to tell you, is hilarious.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.470
Mina's tweet became a viral moment

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:17.250
and an incredibly popular meme.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:19.440
So, yes it's true that Lizzo didn't invent

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:21.240
the phrase but does that prevent her

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.250
from filing a trademark?

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:24.090
Not really.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.920
This isn't a copyright
claim where originality

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:29.680
and creativity are incredibly important.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:31.860
And, where you can use
that kind of protection

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.740
to stop another person
from stealing their work.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.850
That phrase is probably so short

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:39.460
that it wouldn't receive
copyright protection.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:40.350
So, the verdict?

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.510
Well, in trademark law
we're most interested

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:44.510
in whether a person has claimed a mark

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:47.410
as their own by using it in commerce.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.150
Mina had a viral moment
but didn't do anything

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:52.400
with it as far as goods or services go.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.410
She didn't start a company with that name,

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:56.470
she didn't slap it on some T-shirts,

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:59.170
or even use it in the
title of her next album.

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:02.170
So, that probably means
it's fair game for Lizzo,

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:04.120
provided that she actually uses it

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:07.500
for a bonafide reason in goods or services

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:08.760
that she provides.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:11.380
Now, Tom Terrific Brady
is great, isn't he?

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:12.790
Or, if you don't live in Boston

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:15.320
maybe you just think he's 100% that bitch.

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:17.730
Either way, he's not the perfect athlete.

